34: \abstract{%
35: The self-dual five-form field strength in ten dimensions carries a large ring of
36: Lorentz invariants, of which only a handful have been written down explicitly.
37: We determine the degree-ten graded piece of that ring exactly. Working over
38: finite fields with certified linear algebra, we construct a fourteen-dimensional
39: atlas of degree-ten invariants and resolve its internal structure completely:
40: the two-dimensional subspace of products of lower-degree invariants, the
41: twelve-dimensional span of the published candidate structures, the
42: twelve-dimensional span of the graph generators, and the eleven-dimensional
43: subspace reachable by the stress-tensor flow that motivates these theories. The
44: resulting quotient is three-dimensional --- exactly so over the rationals, not
45: only modulo a prime --- and we give explicit compact representatives for it. We show that two decompositions which both read twelve
46: plus two are not the same decomposition: the published span contains one product
47: direction, so its non-product content is eleven, and we write that direction's
48: generator down. The two dimensions that a computation over finite fields could
49: not settle are established exactly over the rationals. Independently, we implement a
50: bridge between the tensor description and a spinor-variable description, fixing
51: every convention explicitly and verifying the map is an isomorphism onto the
52: gamma-traceless symmetric chiral square. The two descriptions are then evaluated
53: at a common registry of five-forms and their invariant spans compared directly.
54: We also replace the floating-point Jacobian rank computation used previously by
55: an exact modular one over the complete candidate selection. Because the
56: coordinate basis is integral, the resulting rank is an unconditional lower bound
57: in characteristic zero rather than a tolerance-dependent estimate, and it is
58: witnessed by an explicit non-vanishing minor; it meets the analytic count of
59: eighty-one from below.%
60: }
61: 
62: \keywords{Extended Supersymmetry, p-branes, Differential and Algebraic Geometry}
