356: \subsection{The quotient over the rationals, not only modulo $p$}
357: \label{sec:czero}
358: 
359: Every dimension above is a rank computed over $\mathbb{F}_p$, and for most of
360: them that costs nothing: a space spanned by exactly $k$ explicit invariants has
361: $\dim_{\mathbb{Q}} \le k$ for free, so a modular rank of $k$ pins the
362: dimension over $\mathbb{Q}$ with no prime excluded. This covers $A_{10}$,
363: $P_{10}$, $G_{10}$ and $B_{10}$.
364: 
365: $D_{10}$ is the exception, and it is the one that matters. It is not given by a
366: spanning set; it is assembled by a closure that admits a generated flow direction
367: when that direction raises the rank \emph{modulo $p$}. A direction rejected mod
368: $p$ still lies in $D_{10}$ and could be independent over $\mathbb{Q}$. So
369: $\dimDten$ bounded $\dim_{\mathbb{Q}} D_{10}$ only from below, and
370: $\dim_{\mathbb{Q}} Q_{10} \le \dimQten$ was all that followed.
371: 
372: Running the same fixed point in exact rational arithmetic removes the modular
373: admission test, and with it the gap. The obstacle is that the flow targets are
374: not all integral: of the targets at degrees four through ten,
375: $\czIntegerRows$ have identical integer coordinates at every prime and
376: $\czLiftedRows$ are reductions of rationals. Those were lifted by CRT across
377: $\czFitPrimes$ primes followed by rational reconstruction, and every
378: reconstructed entry was then required to reduce correctly at a held-out
379: prime~$\czHoldoutPrime$ that took no part in the lift. No lift failed and no
380: held-out reduction disagreed.
381: 
382: The closure then reaches its fixed point in $\czSweeps$ sweeps under exact
383: rational arithmetic, giving
384: \begin{equation}
385:   \dim_{\mathbb{Q}} D_{10} = \dimDtenQ ,
386:   \qquad
387:   \dim_{\mathbb{Q}} Q_{10} \czQtenRelation \dimQtenQ .
388:   \label{eq:czero}
389: \end{equation}
390: This is an equality and not a bound, because at a fixed point reached over
391: $\mathbb{Q}$ no generated direction raises the rank over $\mathbb{Q}$: the
392: reached span \emph{is} $D_{10}$. No separate upper-bound argument is needed --- the
393: upper bound was an artefact of computing in $\mathbb{F}_p$. As an independently
394: checkable witness the certificate records an explicit
395: $\czMinorSize \times \czMinorSize$ minor of the reached span whose integer
396: determinant, computed by fraction-free elimination, is non-zero.
397: 
398: \paragraph{The one analytic input.} The closure assigns each generated target
399: to a graded piece using the leading field degree of its generator, and that

