607: \paragraph{Degrees four, six and ten agree; degree eight does not, and that is a
608: result.} At degrees four, six and ten the two evaluation spans are equal, with
609: the change-of-basis map validated on holdout samples that took no part in the
610: fit. At degree ten in particular both sides reach $\traceRankDegTen$ and the
611: spans coincide, which is the cross-validation this comparison was built to test.
612: 
613: At degree eight the spinor-side port-graph stream reaches
614: $\spinorRankDegEight$ where the tensor side reaches $\traceRankDegEight$, and the
615: containment is strict: every spinor-side invariant lies in the tensor-side span,
616: but one tensor-side direction is not reached. This is not a defect of the bridge,
617: which is verified to be an isomorphism, nor of the sampling, which had saturated.
618: It is a property of the port-graph ansatz: single-graph contractions of the
619: invariant tensor miss a degree-eight direction.
620: 
621: This is not an inference. Running the spinor enumeration with its
622: stopping rule disabled, so that it terminates on candidate exhaustion rather than
623: on an externally supplied target, reaches rank seven at degree eight --- but only
624: when a separate family of structured tensor-word candidates is included alongside
625: the port graphs. Our exact modular computation re-implements the port graphs and
626: not that family, and lands on six. The two numbers are consistent, and together
627: they locate the missing direction precisely: it is not reachable by any
628: single-graph contraction.
629: 
630: This can be made sharper than ``the two numbers are consistent''. Re-running the
631: degree-eight comparison with the \emph{full} spinor candidate family --- port
632: graphs, products of lower degrees, the archive's structured candidates, and
633: tensor words, between $\dEightSpinorRowsMin$ and $\dEightSpinorRowsMax$ rows
634: depending on the prime --- gives
635: \begin{equation}
636:   \text{trace rank} = \text{spinor rank} = \text{union rank}
637:   = \dEightUnionRank
638: \end{equation}
639: at all $\dEightPrimes$ primes tested, with two-way containment and a
640: change-of-basis map validated on holdout samples. The union rank is the
641: load-bearing number: two \emph{different} seven-dimensional subspaces would also
642: give equal ranks, and a union of eight; the union being $\dEightUnionRank$ is
643: what makes this span equality rather than dimension agreement.
644: 
645: Dropping each spinor family in turn and re-ranking then isolates the
