1: \section{The exact degree-ten flow closure}
2: \label{app:closure}
3: 
4: \paragraph{Targets and generators.}
5: At degrees four through ten the closure consumes exactly the generators
6: \begin{widetext}
7: \begin{equation}
8: \operatorname{Tr}\tau,\;
9: \operatorname{Tr}\tau_2,\;
10: \operatorname{Tr}\tau_3,\;
11: \operatorname{Tr}\tau_4,\;
12: \operatorname{Tr}\tau_5,\;
13: (\operatorname{Tr}\tau)^2,\;
14: \operatorname{Tr}\tau\operatorname{Tr}\tau_2,\;
15: \operatorname{Tr}\tau\operatorname{Tr}\tau_3,\;
16: (\operatorname{Tr}\tau_2)^2,\;
17: \operatorname{Tr}\tau_2\operatorname{Tr}\tau_3 .
18: \end{equation}
19: \end{widetext}
20: This list was determined by instrumenting the closure rather than by reading the
21: construction, so that a generator consumed but not documented would show up.
22: 
23: \paragraph{Activation rules.}
24: A target is \emph{generated} when the flow produces it formally, and
25: \emph{activated} when the leading field degree of the generating expression is
26: low enough for it to contribute at the degree under consideration. The leading
27: degrees follow the rule: $\operatorname{Tr}(\tau)$ has leading field degree four
28: --- not two, by theorem~\ref{thm:gten} --- while $\operatorname{Tr}(\tau^k)$ has
29: $2k$ for $k \ge 2$, and products add. All generators were checked against this
30: rule independently at every prime, with no exceptions.
31: 
32: \paragraph{Why the distinction is the whole result.}
33: The span of \emph{generated} targets has dimension $\dimAten$, equal to
34: $\dim\Ften$. The span of \emph{activated} targets, closed under iteration, has
35: dimension $\dimDtenQ$. Identifying the two returns $\dim\Qten = 0$. The raw span
36: is retained in the test suite as a negative fixture for exactly this reason.
37: 
38: \paragraph{Fixed-point algorithm.}
39: Starting from the degree-four seed, repeatedly: generate targets from the current
40: span, admit those whose leading degree permits activation, and re-rank. Terminate
41: when a sweep raises no rank. Termination occurred after $\czSweeps$ sweeps.
42: 
43: \paragraph{The exact rational calculation.}
44: Of the closure's target rows, $\czIntegerRows$ were already integral. The
45: remaining $\czLiftedRows$ were reconstructed as follows: evaluate at
46: $\czFitPrimes$ fitting primes, combine by the Chinese remainder theorem, and
47: recover a rational by rational reconstruction~\cite{Wang:1982}. The reconstructed
48: rows were then validated at the held-out prime $\czHoldoutPrime$, which took no
49: part in the fit; there were no mismatches. The closure was then run entirely in
50: exact $\mathbb{Q}$ arithmetic.
51: 
52: \paragraph{Dimensions by degree.}
53: Over $\Q$ the reachable dimensions are $1$, $2$, $7$ and $\dimDtenQ$ at degrees
54: four, six, eight and ten.
55: 
56: \paragraph{Lower-bound certificate.}
57: An explicit $\czMinorSize \times \czMinorSize$ integer minor of the reachable
58: span has non-zero determinant. The submatrix, the column set, the integer scale
59: factor by which the rows were cleared of denominators, and the full integer
60: determinant are recorded in the artifact. The determinant was computed by Bareiss
61: elimination~\cite{Bareiss:1968} and confirmed by an independent exact rational
62: routine.
63: 
64: \paragraph{Upper bound: why none is needed.}
65: Running the closure over $\Q$ removes the modular admission test that made the
66: earlier figure a lower bound. At the fixed point, no further generated direction
67: raises the rank over $\Q$; therefore the reached span \emph{is} $\Dten$, and
68: $\dim_\Q\Dten = \dimDtenQ$ is an equality. This is the substantive difference
69: between the present computation and its modular predecessor.
70: 
71: \paragraph{Quotient maps.}
72: Three exact annihilating covectors are exhibited, each vanishing on $\Dten$ and
73: independent on $\Ften$. They give the quotient map to $\Qten$ directly, and the
74: quotient representatives are indexed by the free columns
75: (table~\ref{tab:quotient}).

