1: \section{Reconstruction of the published span}
2: \label{app:b10}
3: 
4: \paragraph{The problem.}
5: The published degree-ten structures are given as explicit expressions. To compare
6: their span with the product subspace over $\Q$ --- rather than modulo a prime ---
7: their coordinate vectors must be lifted to $\Q$.
8: 
9: \paragraph{CRT and rational reconstruction.}
10: Each published structure was evaluated at $\bpFitPrimes$ fitting primes. The
11: residues were combined by the Chinese remainder theorem into a residue modulo the
12: product, and a rational with bounded numerator and denominator was recovered by
13: rational reconstruction~\cite{Wang:1982}. All $\dimBten$ published structures
14: lifted; none failed.
15: 
16: \paragraph{Held-out validation.}
17: The reconstructed rationals were reduced modulo the held-out prime
18: $\bpHoldoutPrime$, which took no part in the fit, and compared against a direct
19: evaluation there. There were no mismatches. This is the step that distinguishes a
20: reconstruction from a guess: rational reconstruction always returns
21: \emph{something}, and only a prime outside the fit can tell you whether it
22: returned the right thing.
23: 
24: \paragraph{Exact intersection.}
25: With the lifts in hand,
26: \begin{equation}
27: \dim_\Q \Bten = \dimBten,\quad
28: \dim_\Q \Pten = \dimPten,\quad
29: \dim_\Q(\Bten + \Pten) = \dimBplusP,
30: \end{equation}
31: whence $\dim_\Q(\Bten \cap \Pten) = \dimBcapP$ by inclusion--exclusion.
32: 
33: \paragraph{The explicit generator.}
34: The one-dimensional intersection is generated by
35: \begin{equation}
36: \begin{split}
37: -55\,I^{(1)} - 252\,I^{(2)}& + 4200\,I^{(5)} + 5040\,I^{(6)} \\
38:  &=\; -1040\,I^{(4)}_1 I^{(6)}_1,
39: \end{split}
40: \end{equation}
41: an identity between integer combinations, verified at the fresh prime
42: $\bpFreshPrime$ on $\bpFreshSamples$ independently drawn samples that took no
43: part in either the fit or the holdout.
44: 
45: \paragraph{Normalisation, and a check that failed.}
46: The verification initially failed at the fresh prime on all six samples. The
47: cause was in the checking code: the two sides of the identity were each being
48: reduced by their own greatest common divisor before comparison, which destroys
49: precisely the relative scale the identity asserts. Recomputed on a single common
50: scale, the identity holds on all six samples.
51: 
52: We record the failure rather than only the eventual success. A check that fails,
53: is adjusted, and then passes is worthless unless the adjustment is stated, because
54: the adjustment is where the error would hide. The adjustment here was to stop
55: normalising the two sides independently; no coefficient was altered to make the
56: identity fit.
57: 
58: \paragraph{Why the two decompositions differ.}
59: The published structures were presented as explicit expressions, not as a basis
60: with specified relations, and there is no reason a set chosen for explicitness
61: should avoid the product sector. That it meets it in exactly one dimension is a
62: structural observation about the published choice, and the direction of the
63: finding --- that $\Bten$ has one dimension \emph{fewer} of primitive content than
64: a naive reading would suggest --- can only weaken a claim about the published
65: span, never strengthen one. No result in this paper depends on the equality.

FILE manuscript/prd/appendices/app_g_rank81.tex 58 83
58: \text{generic functional rank} \;\ge\; \charZeroLowerBound .
59: \end{equation}
60: No prime is excluded and no genericity assumption on the sample point is needed:
61: a specific point exhibiting rank $\charZeroLowerBound$ bounds the generic rank
62: from below.
63: 
64: \paragraph{The upper bound.}
65: The matching upper bound $\genericFunctionalDim$ is analytic, following from the
66: generic orbit dimension of the Lorentz action on
67: $\Lambda^5_+$~\cite{Hutomo:2025chiral}. We cite it and do not reprove it. The
68: combination of the two gives the exact value, and we are explicit about which
69: half is ours.
70: 
71: \paragraph{Scope.}
72: The certified statement is that \emph{the selected family has generic functional
73: rank $\genericFunctionalDim$}. It is not the statement that all
74: $\exactJacScheduled$ candidates are algebraically independent: functional
75: independence at a generic point and algebraic independence are different
76: properties, and only the former is established here.
